      {
        "id": "story",
        "type": "story",
        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "How GPS finds you"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "A GPS receiver uses three satellites to fix your latitude, longitude and altitude. Behind this is a system of three equations in three unknowns."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "equation of a plane",
          "three possibilities",
          "step 1",
          "step 2",
          "step 3",
          "special cases",
          "word problems"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "A linear equation in three variables has the form ax plus by plus cz plus d equals 0 and represents a plane in space.\nA system of three such equations can meet at one point, along a line, or not at all.\nStep one: choose two equations and make the coefficient of one variable numerically equal.\nStep two: eliminate that variable from two different pairs of equations to get two equations in two variables.\nStep three: solve the two-variable system, then substitute to find the third variable.\nIf you reach a false statement like 0 = 1, there is no solution; if you reach an identity 0 = 0, there are infinitely many.\nWord problems become systems when we name the unknowns and write one equation per condition."
        }
      },
